This study examines an oncolytic virotherapy model described by a doubly haptotactic cross-diffusion system:
\begin{equation*}
  \left\{\begin{array}{ll}
  u_t=D_u\Delta u-\xi_u\nabla\cdot(u\nabla v)+\mu_u u(1-u)-\rho uz,
  v_t=- (\alpha_u u+\alpha_w w)v,\\ w_t=D_w\Delta w-\xi_w\nabla\cdot(w\nabla
v)- w+\rho uz,\\ z_t=D_z\Delta z-\delta_z z- \rho uz+\beta w,
  \end{array}\right. \end{equation*}
where the parameters $D_u,D_w,D_z,\xi_u,\xi_w,\delta_z,\rho$, $\alpha_u,\alpha_w,\mu_u,\beta$ are positive. Under no-flux boundary conditions on a smoothly bounded domain $\Omega\subset {\mathbb{R}}^2$ and with suitably regular initial data, the global existence of a classical solution was previously established by Tao and Winkler (2020). Utilizing an appropriate quasi-Lyapunov functional, it is demonstrated that if the virus replication rate satisfies $\beta<1$, the global classical solution $(u,v,w,z)$ remains uniformly bounded and converges exponentially to the steady state $(1, 0, 0, 0)$ within the $(L^\infty(\Omega))^4$ topology as $t\rightarrow \infty$.

This study investigates the equation featuring a higher-order fractional Laplacian:
\begin{equation*} \left\{\begin{aligned}
&(-\Delta)^{p+{\frac{\alpha}{2}}}u(x)=u_+^\gamma~~ \mbox{ in }\mathbb{R}^n,\\
&\int_{\mathbb{R}^n}u_+^\gamma dx<+\infty, \end{aligned}\right. \end{equation*}
where $p\geq 1$ is an integer, $0<\alpha<2$, $n> 2p+\alpha$, and $\gamma \in (1,\frac{n}{n-2p-\alpha})$. We derive an integral representation formula for any nonconstant classical solution that adheres to specific growth conditions at infinity. Utilizing the method of moving planes in integral forms, we demonstrate that such solutions exhibit radial symmetry about a specific point in $\mathbb{R}^n$ and are monotonically decreasing along the radial direction.

The LHCb detector has observed the decays $ \Lambda_b^0 \to \psi(2S)pK^-$ and $ \Lambda_b^0 \to J/\psi \pi^+ \pi^- pK^-$ using a dataset with an integrated luminosity of 3 fb$^{-1}$ from proton-proton collisions at 7 and 8 TeV center-of-mass energies. The $\psi(2S)$ mesons were identified via the decay channels $\psi(2S)\to\mu^+\mu^-$ and $\psi(2S)\to J/\psi\pi^+\pi^-$. The branching fractions for these processes, normalized to that of $ \Lambda_b^0 \to J/\psi pK^-$, are determined as:
\begin{eqnarray*}
  \frac{\mathcal{B}(\Lambda_b^0 \to \psi(2S) pK^-)}
  {\mathcal{B}(\Lambda_b^0 \to J/\psi pK^-)}
  & = & (20.70\pm 0.76\pm 0.46\pm 0.37)\times10^{-2} \,,
  \frac{\mathcal{B}(\Lambda_b^0 \to J/\psi \pi^+ \pi^- pK^-)}
  {\mathcal{B}(\Lambda_b^0 \to J/\psi pK^-)}
  & = & (20.86\pm 0.96\pm 1.34)\times10^{-2} \,, \end{eqnarray*}
where the first uncertainties are statistical, the second are systematic, and the third arises from uncertainties in the known branching fractions of $J/\psi$ and $\psi(2S)$. Additionally, the mass of the $\Lambda_b^0$ baryon is measured to be $$ M(\Lambda_b^0) = 5619.65 \pm 0.17 \pm 0.17\mathrm{MeV}/c^2,$$ with the errors representing statistical and systematic contributions, respectively.

The semileptonic decay $B^{+}\to p\overline{p}\mu^{+}\nu_{\mu}$, which is suppressed by the Cabibbo factor, has been observed for the first time. This observation is based on data from $pp$ collisions with integrated luminosities of 1.0, 2.0, and 1.7 fb$^{-1}$ at center-of-mass energies of 7, 8, and 13 TeV, respectively. The differential branching fraction is determined as a function of the $p\overline{p}$ invariant mass, utilizing the $B^{+}\to J/\psi K^{+}$ decay mode for normalization. The total branching fraction is measured to be \begin{align*}
  \mathcal{B}(B^{+}\to p\overline{p}\mu^{+}\nu_{\mu}) = (5.27 ^{+0.23}_{-0.24}
\pm 0.21 \pm 0.15)\times 10^{-6}, \end{align*} where the first error represents the statistical uncertainty, the second is systematic, and the third arises from the uncertainty in the branching fraction of the normalization channel.

Let $\mathcal{S}^*_t(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$ represent the class of functions $f$ that are analytic within the open unit disc $\Delta$, normalized such that $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)-1=0$, and which satisfy the two-sided inequality:
\begin{equation*}
  -\frac{\pi\alpha_1}{2}< \arg\left\{\frac{zf'(z)}{f(z)}\right\}
  <\frac{\pi\alpha_2}{2} \quad (z\in\Delta), \end{equation*}
with $0<\alpha_1,\alpha_2\leq1$. This class $\mathcal{S}^*_t(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$ constitutes a subclass of strongly starlike functions of order $\beta$, where $\beta=\max\{\alpha_1,\alpha_2\}$. The primary goal of this paper is to establish specific inequalities, including upper and lower bounds for ${\rm Re}\{zf'(z)/f(z)\}$, a growth theorem, logarithmic coefficient estimates, and coefficient estimates for functions $f$ within the class $\mathcal{S}^*_t(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$.

This paper examines a Cauchy problem associated with the four-dimensional energy-critical heat equation:
\begin{equation*} \begin{cases} u_t=\Delta u+u^{3}
~&\mbox{ in }~ {\mathbb R}^4 \times (0,\infty),\\ u(x,0)=u_0(x) ~&\mbox{ in }~
{\mathbb R}^4. \end{cases} \end{equation*}
We establish the existence of a positive solution $u(x,t)$ that undergoes infinite-time blow-up with the rate $\| u(\cdot,t)\|_{L^\infty({\mathbb R}^4)} \sim \ln t$ as $t \to \infty$, and we demonstrate the stability of this blow-up phenomenon. These findings provide a rigorous confirmation of Conjecture 1.1 proposed by Fila and King \cite[Conjecture 1.1]{filaking12}.

A set of finite points in $\mathbb{R}^d$ is defined as being in general position when no $d + 1$ points are coplanar on a single hyperplane. We define $\alpha_d(N)$ as the maximum integer such that every collection of $N$ points in $\mathbb{R}^d$, provided no $d + 2$ points share a hyperplane, necessarily includes a subset of size $\alpha_d(N)$ in general position. Employing hypergraph container techniques, Balogh and Solymosi previously established that $\alpha_2(N) < N^{5/6 + o(1)}$. This work extends their methodology to derive improved upper bounds for $\alpha_d(N)$ where $d \geq 3$. Specifically, for odd dimensions $d$, we prove $\alpha_d(N) < N^{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2(d + 1)} + o(1)}$, while for even dimensions, the bound is $\alpha_d(N) < N^{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{d-1} + o(1)}$. Additionally, we investigate the classic problem of finding the maximum count $a(d,k,n)$ of points chosen from the grid $[n]^d$ such that no $k + 2$ points lie on a $k$-flat. For fixed $d$ and $k$, we demonstrate that
\begin{equation*}
  a(d,k,n)\leq O\left(n^{\frac{d}{2\lfloor
(k+2)/4\rfloor}(1-\frac{1}{2\lfloor(k+2)/4\rfloor d+1})}\right),
\end{equation*}
which refines the earlier best-known bound of $O\left(n^{\frac{d}{\lfloor (k + 2)/2\rfloor}}\right)$ attributed to Lefmann, specifically when $k+2 \equiv 0$ or $1 \pmod 4$.

This work provides a comprehensive review of exact static and time-dependent solutions within Jackiw-Teitelboim (JT) gravity, with a specific focus on one-parameter solutions for the metric and dilaton fields in static Poincaré coordinates. The study investigates cosmological toy models where the time-dependent metric asymptotically approaches AdS hyperbolic spacetime within a non-static patch. In these scenarios, the complete dilaton profile is formulated as a real-time Laplace transform over the spatial coordinate $z$ in the real Laplace domain $s$. The resulting time-dependent Laplace amplitude, $	ilde{\phi}(t,s)$, satisfies a linear ordinary differential equation and can be expressed as a linear combination of associated Legendre functions, $P_{1}^{
u(t,s)}(	anh(t))$ and $Q_{1}^{
u(t,s)}(	anh(t))$, featuring complex values for $
u(t,s)$. Furthermore, the spacetime representation of the dilaton profile is described via a Bromwich integral over $s$. The analysis also addresses Birkhoff's theorem, demonstrating that JT theory does not globally support static, time-independent vacuum solutions. Finally, a viable deformed JT action is examined to confirm the existence of exact analytic solutions in both static and cosmological configurations.
